ly understood. The pattern repeats because there was no enforcement gate when PR #3249 fixed impact-analyst \u2014 it was fixed in isolation without auditing sibling agents or documenting the required pattern.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 6-sigma frequency analysis via backlog history search\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Agent spawned via `Agent()` tool completes analysis but findings silently vanish because instructions assume synchronous return and never call SendMessage \u2192 Agent explicitly reports findings back to orchestrator via SendMessage (for async dispatch) or backend method (for non-team spawn).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:50:28.405304Z</sub>\\n\\nSCOPE_EXPANSION: 3 additional affected systems beyond the two originally cited (contract-verification.md, implement-feature/SKILL.md): dispatch-contract/SKILL.md (root doctrine authorizing the broken pattern), subagent-contract/SKILL.md (the general contract contract-verification overrides), and groom/error.md (a second, independent bare-Agent() dispatch with the identical bug, in a different workflow).\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \u2014 the broken agent. Line 151: \\\"Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \u2014 you register no artifact and write no task state.\\\" A load-bearing false assumption under bare `Agent()` dispatch. Its `tools:` frontmatter (line 5) still grants `SendMessage` \u2014 the grant was never revoked, only the instruction to use it was removed by commit 11082f0eb. Needs an explicit reporting mechanism restored.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` (step 4a, lines 151-207) \u2014 spawns `Agent(subagent_type=\\\"dh:contract-verification\\\", ...)` as a bare, non-TeamCreate, non-task-worker call (line 163), then line 187 treats the result as synchronous (\\\"If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block...\\\"). Under this runtime's async non-fork `Agent()` dispatch, that check always sees nothing \u2014 contract concerns are silently dropped every time, with no error signal. This is the direct symptom the item describes.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` (Agent Failure procedure, lines 15-33) \u2014 a second, independent bare `Agent(subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\", ...)` dispatch (line 18) whose result (`last_success`, `first_failure`, `failure_type`) is branched on synchronously by a mermaid decision tree in Step 4. Same defect class, different workflow. Under async dispatch this diagnostic branch is unreachable \u2014 the \\\"2nd failure -> Escalation\\\" workflow-block logic is currently dead code.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\nNone identified beyond the producer/consumer/agent-instruction entries above.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\nNone identified \u2014 no user-facing docs describe this dispatch contract; the affected instructions are agent/skill files, covered under Agent Instructions below.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 `<dispatch_selection>` states: \\\"A dispatch that hands over no dh operation \u2014 an independent review whose finding is its response \u2014 has nothing here to reach: any specialist that fits runs it, dh roster or not.\\\" This doctrine legitimizes the exact broken pattern \u2014 it documents \\\"finding is my response\\\" as a valid dispatch category, when under this runtime's actual async non-fork `Agent()` semantics it never is. Until this changes, contract-verification is the reference implementation of a codified anti-pattern that future agent authors will reproduce.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 `<result_destination>` (correct in itself): \\\"Put your result where your own agent file says... Where neither names one: deliverables go in repository files; every other document is an artifact, registered with `artifact_register`... Hand the next step a plan, task, or artifact id.\\\" This is the fallback contract-verification's own file opts out of via an explicit override. The fix likely means either removing contract-verification's override (fall back to this artifact-based contract) or adding an equally explicit, dispatch-context-correct override.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n1. `contract-verification.md` \u2014 producer, HIGH risk, broken today (silent no-op)\\n2. `implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u00a74a \u2014 consumer, HIGH risk, broken today\\n3. `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 agent-instruction / root doctrine, HIGH risk (actively misleading once contract-verification is fixed unless updated in the same change)\\n4. `subagent-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 agent-instruction, LOW-MEDIUM risk (override clause is technically correct, just exploited by contract-verification's opt-out)\\n5. `impact-analyst.md` \u2014 sibling agent, comparison baseline only, not itself broken. Fixed in PR #3249 (commit 43058f345) via `SendMessage(to=\\\"team-lead\\\", ...)` \u2014 but impact-analyst is dispatched via `TeamCreate`, where \\\"team-lead\\\" is a real addressable teammate. contract-verification is dispatched via bare `Agent()` from inside a skill run with no team-lead teammate to address, so a verbatim copy of this fix would not resolve correctly.\\n6. `t0-baseline-capture.md` / `tn-verification-gate.md` \u2014 precedent, not broken. Register results via `artifact_register` (types `T0-baseline`, `TN-verification`); the consuming step reads them back via `artifact_read` rather than depending on the dispatch call's return value. Strongest available precedent for the fix.\\n7. `groom/error.md` \\\"Agent Failure\\\" procedure \u2014 consumer, MEDIUM-HIGH risk, same defect class, different workflow (see Consumers above).\\n8. `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 verified clean. Zero bare `Agent(` calls (grep confirmed). Its Progress Loop dispatches via delegation prompts that instruct the agent to call `artifact_register` itself and return a `STATUS: DONE / path:` marker; the orchestrator then verifies via `artifact list --item-id {issue} --artifact-type {type}` and explicitly must NOT call `artifact_register` itself as a workaround. Correct pattern, should not be touched by this fix.\\n\\nNo test file references `contract-verification`, its `<concerns>` output contract, or the groom `error.md` diagnostic dispatch \u2014 verification risk is HIGH for both consumer sites: this defect has no automated guard and surfaces only as \\\"contract concerns never appear in backlog items,\\\" a silent absence that's easy to miss.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible \u2014 contract-verification.md needs an explicit reporting mechanism restored\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface \u2014 implement-feature/SKILL.md \u00a74a and groom/error.md's Agent Failure procedure both need to read the result from wherever it now lands\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated \u2014 N/A, no user-facing docs affected\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated \u2014 dispatch-contract/SKILL.md's \\\"finding is my response\\\" category needs qualifying or removing (or scoping explicitly to dispatch contexts where a synchronous/addressable return actually holds)\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing) \u2014 N/A\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated \u2014 N/A, none identified\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T15:03:40.356630Z</sub>\\n\\n**MISSION_ALIGNED**\\n\\nThe user-provided direction \u2014 route `dh:contract-verification` findings through backend operations (`backlog_groom` or `artifact_register`) rather than `SendMessage` or synchronous response \u2014 is aligned with and extends the established architectural principle set by commit `11082f0eb` (\\\"refactor(development-harness): route agent handoffs through the backend, not SendMessage (#3202)\\\").\\n\\n## Architectural Principle\\n\\nCommit `11082f0eb` established a sweeping refactoring with explicit rationale:\\n\\n> SendMessage is a Claude-Code-only tool. This plugin's shipped instruction content also targets Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Kilo, where an agent has no such tool, so every instruction to use it is unexecutable there. Data transport now goes through the backend, with the destination named at the point the data is written and read back by the consuming step.\\n\\nThe commit cites concrete examples of this pattern:\\n- Four perspective reviewers write verdict blocks to the Review Results section of their own SAM task (not via SendMessage)\\n- The dispatch blocker-relay loop appends orchestrator context to the blocked task (not via SendMessage)\\n- The grooming swarm's section-writing agents (classifier, rtica-assessor, alignment-analyst, impact-analyst) confirm their sections are readable and written via `backlog_groom`, replacing earlier SendMessage broadcasts\\n\\n**Core Principle**: Data transport is routed through the backend (task state, artifact registry, item sections) with the destination named at write time. The consuming step reads it back via the same backend operation.\\n\\n## Alignment Analysis\\n\\nThe user-provided direction fits this principle exactly:\\n\\n1. **Backend routing, not SendMessage**: \\\"write results directly to the backend (e.g. `backlog_groom` on the Concerns section, or `artifact_register`, mirroring how `dh:t0-baseline-capture` reports) instead of relying on a returned value or on `SendMessage`\\\" \u2014 this is the same pattern established in `11082f0eb`.\\n\\n2. **No artificial team-lead dependency**: The rationale \\\"there is no team-lead when there is no team\\\" is sound. Impact-analyst was fixed via PR #3249 by adding SendMessage(to=\\\"team-lead\\\"), but that fix assumes a TeamCreate dispatch context where a team-lead teammate is addressable. Contract-verification is dispatched via bare `Agent()` from inside a skill (implement-feature), with no team context. Trying to SendMessage to a non-existent team-lead will fail silently or block, reproducing the original defect rather than fixing it.\\n\\n3. **Precedent with t0-baseline-capture / tn-verification-gate**: Both of these agents operate as bare `Agent()` dispatches (not TeamCreate). Both register findings via `artifact_register` and return STATUS: DONE with artifact metadata. The consuming step reads the artifact back via `artifact_read`. This is the exact pattern the user proposes for contract-verification, and it is already proven working in this codebase.\\n\\n4. **Dispatch-contract doctrine alignment**: The current `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` states \\\"A dispatch that hands over no dh operation \u2014 an independent review whose finding is its response \u2014 has nothing here to reach.\\\" This doctrine authorizes the broken pattern (finding is the response). The proposed fix aligns with the architectural direction by making findings go to backend instead, which means the doctrine must be updated alongside the agent fix \u2014 the dispatch-selection section needs to be qualified to exclude bare `Agent()` dispatch contexts, or updated to reference the subagent-contract fallback (`artifact_register` for \\\"every other document\\\").\\n\\n5. **Subagent-contract fallback applies**: The subagent-contract skill states the fallback: \\\"Where neither the agent file nor dispatch names a destination: deliverables the repository keeps go in repository files; every other document is an artifact, registered with `artifact_register`.\\\" Contract concerns are a document for consumption by a downstream verification step (implement-feature step 4a and the consuming orchestrator), so they fall under \\\"every other document\\\" \u2014 artifact_register is the correct routing by the fallback contract.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n- **Commit establishing the principle**: `11082f0eb` (\\\"refactor(development-harness): route agent handoffs through the backend, not SendMessage (#3202)\\\") \u2014 established that backend routing is the general architectural direction for cross-harness portability.\\n- **Proof of concept for bare Agent() backend routing**: `/plugins/development-harness/agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` and `/plugins/development-harness/agents/tn-verification-gate.md` \u2014 both dispatch via bare `Agent()` and route results via `artifact_register`, with consumers reading via `artifact_read`.\\n- **Architectural contracts**: `/plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` and `/plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 define the architecture and fallback pattern.\\n- **Proof that follow-up SendMessage fix is context-dependent**: Commit `43058f345` (\\\"fix(development-harness): add SendMessage completion report to impact-analyst (#3249)\\\") added SendMessage only because impact-analyst is dispatched via TeamCreate where team-lead is addressable \u2014 the commit notes \\\"When not operating as a teammate (no team-lead present), end your response with the same `STATUS: DONE` block as your final output instead,\\\" revealing that SendMessage is a team-context pattern, not a general pattern.\\n\\n## Design Decision Context\\n\\nThe proposed fix routes through backend rather than SendMessage for three reasons:\\n1. **Architectural consistency**: Extends the established principle from `11082f0eb` \u2014 backend routing over tool-specific mechanisms.\\n2. **Dispatch context mismatch**: contract-verification is bare `Agent()` dispatch, not TeamCreate, so no team-lead is present to receive SendMessage.\\n3. **Proven precedent**: t0-baseline-capture and tn-verification-gate show this pattern is working in the same codebase for the same dispatch context.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-28/29, while running /dh:implement-feature on plan Pd95e593c (#3151), dispatching dh:contract-verification for task T1\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:50:36.568270Z</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 6-sigma frequency analysis via backlog history search\\n\\n**Frequency**: 2+ confirmed occurrences, plus 4+ additional instances identified in audit #3250\\n\\n**Matches**:\\n- #3248 \u2014 \\\"fix: impact-analyst agent never sends a completion message back to the orchestrator\\\" \u2014 2026-08 (open)\\n- #3276 \u2014 \\\"fix: contract-verification agent's findings never reach the orchestrator under async subagent dispatch\\\" \u2014 2026-08 (open, current)\\n\\n**Pattern**: Multiple `dh:*` agents were written with the assumption that their final text output would be returned synchronously to the caller, but the `Agent()` tool provides async dispatch where final text is invisible \u2014 agents must call `SendMessage` to reach the orchestrator. This assumption persists across at least 6 agents in the codebase (impact-analyst, contract-verification, rtica-assessor, classifier, backlog-item-groomer, alignment-analyst).\\n\\n**Improvement**: Establish a documentation-and-review gate requiring all `dh:*` agents dispatched via `Agent()` tool to include an explicit completion-report step in their output format (calling `SendMessage(to=\\\"main\\\", message=...)` or writing findings via backend method like `artifact_register` / `backlog_groom`), and audit all existing agents against this requirement in a follow-up chore.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"62f9d5b37ba69d808ce82b1856fd1ea43e5fd397ba25f208634feb410b966047\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRRiiow\"}"}